<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peraea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Perea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peraea was a region east of the Jordan River, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, in what is now Jordan. Settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the Hasmonean period, it was ruled together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> after 4 BCE and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a> before passing to direct Roman rule. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> was executed at its fortress of Machaerus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caligula added Galilee and <b>Peraea</b> in 39, and Claudius, whom Agrippa had helped to the throne, gave him Judea and Samaria in 41, restoring his grandfather's kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Herod the Great in 4 BCE, Augustus gave his son Herod Antipas two separate territories: Galilee, west of the Sea of Galilee, and <b>Peraea</b>, a strip east of the Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</a></li></ul></section>
